{
  "dom_id": "step-q39378",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)={x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+x+7.$ Given that $f(-7)=0,$ factor $f(x)$ over the real numbers.",
  "worked_solution": "If $f(-7)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x+7)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x+7)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+7)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+7)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-7$ | $1$ | $7$ | $1$ | $7$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-7$ | $0$ | $-7$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+x+7}{x+7}=({x}^{2}+1),\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+x+7=(x+7)({x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+1$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={0}^{2}-4(1)(1)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+1$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n(x+7)({x}^{2}+1).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
